WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.110 --> 00:00:01.110
Vsauce!

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:06.130
Kevin here, and this PENNY BRICK contains
a strictly monotonically increasing sequence

00:00:06.130 --> 00:00:12.381
to DETECT FRAUD that depends in part on an
8-year old genius in the 1700’s and how

00:00:12.381 --> 00:00:16.600
loaded with useless knowledge YOUR brain is.

00:00:16.600 --> 00:00:23.580
It also contains 50 rolls of 50 American pennies,
each of which weighs 2.5 grams and is composed

00:00:23.580 --> 00:00:28.110
of 97.5% zinc and 2.5% copper.

00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:30.220
HERE’S THE PROBLEM.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:37.700
One of these 50 rolls is full of FAKES.

00:00:37.700 --> 00:00:43.250
Which one of you is lookin’ a little SUS...picious?

00:00:43.250 --> 00:00:49.380
The fake could be anywhere.

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:55.680
The only thing I know is that the counterfeit
pennies are .5g lighter than real pennies.

00:00:55.680 --> 00:00:59.420
That’s too light, though, to feel out by
hand.

00:00:59.420 --> 00:01:02.100
So I’ve gotta measure them.

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:03.100
HOW?

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:08.500
I could measure all 50 rolls one by one until
I found the fakes.

00:01:08.500 --> 00:01:12.830
Every roll should be 125g for the pennies
plus 3g more for the wrap.

00:01:12.830 --> 00:01:16.430
It'll work, but it could take up to 50 measurements.

00:01:16.430 --> 00:01:22.510
The weird thing is… there’s actually an
elegant way to measure just ONCE -- TAKE ONE

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:28.910
SINGLE MEASUREMENT -- to find out exactly
which stack is phoney.

00:01:28.910 --> 00:01:33.740
THAT solution is not obvious so let’s start
with the obvious.

00:01:33.740 --> 00:01:40.310
You know you can speed up measuring by, say,
breaking the 50 stacks into 5 groups of 10.

00:01:40.310 --> 00:01:46.090
Measure the 5 groups and 1 will be light -- measure
the 10 stacks in that group until you find

00:01:46.090 --> 00:01:47.090
the fake.

00:01:47.090 --> 00:01:48.340
If you’re super lucky?

00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:54.740
20% chance you get the right group on the
first try, 10% chance it’s the right stack…

00:01:54.740 --> 00:02:02.640
there’s a 2% chance you can do this in just
two measurements, but it could take 15.

00:02:02.640 --> 00:02:10.030
There’s a 100% chance it’ll work, and
you know that -- because it’s in your System

00:02:10.030 --> 00:02:11.030
1.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:16.830
You’ve stored so many patterns and processes
in the dual process model of your brain -- System

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:22.860
1 thinking is the automatic intuition you
build up with knowledge and experience.

00:02:22.860 --> 00:02:27.730
It’s what your brain knows before you even
know you know.

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:32.440
System 2 is the slower, calculated thought
you apply it with.

00:02:32.440 --> 00:02:33.870
HERE’S A QUESTION.

00:02:33.870 --> 00:02:41.410
Which system do you need to measure 50 stacks
of pennies just once and be guaranteed to

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:43.410
find the imposter?

00:02:43.410 --> 00:02:44.720
Both.

00:02:44.720 --> 00:02:45.720
You need both.

00:02:45.720 --> 00:02:55.030
First, I gotta find out what 50 stacks of
pennies SHOULD weigh -- 2.5g each x (50)^2

00:02:55.030 --> 00:02:58.530
= 6,250g = or 6.25kg.

00:02:58.530 --> 00:03:03.920
If fake pennies are half a gram short, a stack
of 50 will be 25g too light.

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:04.920
Cool.

00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:05.920
Now what?

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:07.940
Now you need to know patterns.

00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:09.629
You need to know sequences.

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:13.560
Maybe you got it from studying convergent
and divergent series.

00:03:13.560 --> 00:03:19.120
Maybe it was Euler, maybe it was calculus,
maybe it was projected investment growth or

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:21.240
growing student loan debt.

00:03:21.240 --> 00:03:27.420
But somehow the long arrangement of numbers
and the implications of those sequences and

00:03:27.420 --> 00:03:31.310
series need to be SOMEWHERE in your System
1.

00:03:31.310 --> 00:03:37.310
Like 1 + 2 + 3 + 4 + 5 + 6 plus… it’s
infinite.

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:44.560
And with the Ramanujan summation, that adds
up to -1/12 -- but that’s another video.

00:03:44.560 --> 00:03:45.690
NOT THIS ONE.

00:03:45.690 --> 00:03:50.630
And we’re not adding infinitely, we’re
only going to 50.

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:56.870
And that summation is so simple that even
an 8 year old can do it -- because literally

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:01.430
an 8 year old Carl Gauss discovered the method.

00:04:01.430 --> 00:04:06.159
Carl Gauss is one of history’s greatest
mathematicians -- and as a child he realized

00:04:06.159 --> 00:04:11.549
that any series of adding consecutive numbers
could be done by taking the whole set of numbers

00:04:11.549 --> 00:04:18.109
and dividing it by 2, then multiplying that
result by the first plus the last number.

00:04:18.109 --> 00:04:20.680
So 1 through 50?

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:27.060
(50/2) * (1+50), or 25 * 51 = 1,275.

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:29.930
Thanks, 8 year old Carl.

00:04:29.930 --> 00:04:35.120
When I was 8 years old, I was listening to
Nirvana’s Nevermind on cassette and begging

00:04:35.120 --> 00:04:37.110
my mom for a Sega Genesis.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:43.069
Anyway, if we treat the 50 stacks as potential
consecutive numbers and take 1 penny from

00:04:43.069 --> 00:04:47.889
the first stack, 2 pennies from the second
stack, 3 pennies from the third stack, and

00:04:47.889 --> 00:04:51.240
so on all the way to the 50th stack.

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.139
Our consecutive penny stack series will give
us 1,275 total pennies.

00:04:57.139 --> 00:05:02.300
At 2.5g each minus the wrappers?

00:05:02.300 --> 00:05:03.300
That’s 3,187.5g.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:09.349
When we take one single weight measurement
of our divergent-series-inspired Gaussian

00:05:09.349 --> 00:05:13.369
child-genius pennies, it will be short by
a certain number of grams.

00:05:13.369 --> 00:05:18.949
We’ll know exactly how many pennies of the
1,275 are fakes.

00:05:18.949 --> 00:05:19.949
Because..

00:05:19.949 --> 00:05:22.080
If it’s short 7 grams?

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:23.440
14 fake pennies.

00:05:23.440 --> 00:05:24.840
19 grams?

00:05:24.840 --> 00:05:26.240
38 fakes.

00:05:26.240 --> 00:05:33.000
And since we took the number of pennies corresponding
to the number of stacks -- like taking 13

00:05:33.000 --> 00:05:38.689
pennies from stack #13 -- however many fake
pennies we have in the group actually tell

00:05:38.689 --> 00:05:42.020
us which stack the fakes came out of!

00:05:42.020 --> 00:05:43.479
SO LET’S FIND OUT.

00:05:43.479 --> 00:05:55.599
WHICH OF MY STACKS IS THE FAKE?

00:05:55.599 --> 00:05:58.599
N… none.

00:05:58.599 --> 00:06:00.099
None.

00:06:00.099 --> 00:06:03.119
None of these pennies are fake.

00:06:03.119 --> 00:06:05.389
I got them from the bank.

00:06:05.389 --> 00:06:13.379
I just really wanted to visualize this problem
because it changed my mind about how thinking

00:06:13.379 --> 00:06:16.599
functions.

00:06:16.599 --> 00:06:20.259
We just do not do this stuff every day.

00:06:20.259 --> 00:06:25.449
To stumble on the right process here, you
probably had to have done some real thought

00:06:25.449 --> 00:06:32.039
about consecutive numbers and number series
in the past -- if that’s not in your System

00:06:32.039 --> 00:06:36.789
1, then System 2 doesn’t have a lot to work
with.

00:06:36.789 --> 00:06:38.270
You could just...

00:06:38.270 --> 00:06:43.349
I guess sort of figure it outfit you never
worked with a number series before if your

00:06:43.349 --> 00:06:46.429
name is CARL GAUSS.

00:06:46.429 --> 00:06:48.490
The rest of us need to know things.

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.009
A lot of things.

00:06:50.009 --> 00:06:57.089
And there's no guarantee that what's in your
System 1 will EVER be useful.

00:06:57.089 --> 00:07:02.569
Because it just isn't... until it is.

00:07:02.569 --> 00:07:08.660
Will you need to know specifically how to
reveal a roll of counterfeit coins in a single

00:07:08.660 --> 00:07:11.179
measurement?

00:07:11.179 --> 00:07:12.379
Probably not.

00:07:12.379 --> 00:07:19.569
But what problem will you need to solve using
the number patterns, mathematical processes,

00:07:19.569 --> 00:07:24.460
and even riddle answers that you've acquired
over a lifetime?

00:07:24.460 --> 00:07:31.289
Without a System 1 loaded with math and science,
Archimedes gets in the bath, sees the displacement

00:07:31.289 --> 00:07:33.559
of water correlating with his body, and…

00:07:33.559 --> 00:07:37.330
I don’t know, washes his smelly armpits.

00:07:37.330 --> 00:07:42.659
There would’ve been nothing for System 2
to activate.

00:07:42.659 --> 00:07:49.119
There's NO WAY to know how you’ll put information
and patterns and relationships and knowledge

00:07:49.119 --> 00:07:58.110
together to solve a very real problem in your
future -- but the more information you pack

00:07:58.110 --> 00:08:05.860
into your System 1, the more likely it is
that your System 2 will get the job done.

00:08:05.860 --> 00:08:08.469
And as always…

00:08:08.469 --> 00:08:13.169
I have so many pennies to roll.

00:08:13.169 --> 00:08:13.849
Thanks for watching.

